---
title: Not Not a Gallery
authors: [Kaela Godfrey]
broadcast: 5
order: 4
start: "12:23"
---

Cultural space has long been shaped by artists’ desire to make, show, and experience art, especially when their work doesn't fit the conventions of traditional institutions. In New York, artists have a long history of creating alternative spaces for work that was experimental, unconventional, or as a result of being priced out of expensive galleries. Since the 1980s, galleries such as Pat Hearn Gallery, Fun Gallery, and Gracie Mansion Gallery have created spaces where artists could take risks, test new ideas, and build communities. Artists like Barbara Kruger brought experimental art into public spaces in the 80’s through billboards and other forms of mass communication, challenging where and how art could be experienced and displayed. More recently, projects such as Kurimanzutto’s Sonora 128 have continued this tradition by using existing public infrastructure as a place for artistic expression. Unconventional cultural spaces are not new; they are part of a longer history of artists finding ways to show work on their own terms.

