U-Haul Gallery, Desnivel, and Montez Press Radio continue this idea in different ways. Rather than creating a permanent institution, each project uses an existing space or infrastructure and temporarily redirects it toward cultural production. U-Haul Gallery uses rental moving trucks as temporary exhibition spaces. Founded in New York in 2024, the project responds to the cost and scarcity of conventional gallery space. The truck functions almost as a white box, and although its dimensions and physical limitations establish certain constraints, its relatively neutral interior provides artists with a blank canvas to determine how the work is displayed and experienced. At the same time, its mobility allows exhibitions to move between neighborhoods and communities. Some audiences intentionally follow the gallery through social media, while others may encounter it unexpectedly.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-04.jpg" alt="Aerial photograph of a street with U-Haul Gallery logos placed along both curbs and map pins marking galleries, bars and shops on the surrounding blocks" ancho="completo" /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-05.jpg" alt="Visitors inside the lit box of a U-Haul truck at night, one in a red Elmo costume on the ramp" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-06.jpg" alt="A U-Haul truck opened on a street, people sitting on the sidewalk and standing in the truck looking at the show" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-07.jpg" alt="The lit interior of a U-Haul truck at night with small framed works on its walls, seen from the ramp" /%}
{% /galeria %}

Desnivel takes a more local approach by placing artwork inside neighborhood businesses such as laundromats, bodegas, hardware stores, and nail salons. Each location already has its own circulation, materials, atmosphere, scale, and community of users. Rather than controlling or changing these conditions, Desnivel allows them to shape how the artwork is encountered. The artwork must coexist with the ordinary activities of the business, meaning that the experience of the exhibition changes from one location to another.  This creates a different kind of spontaneity, as Desnivel is already embedded within a pre-existing community. Its cultural spaces are discovered through everyday routines rather than separate from them. Someone doing laundry or buying groceries can encounter an exhibition without planning to visit a cultural space. The businesses are also compensated, creating a relationship between artists, local businesses, and the communities they serve.

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-08.jpg" alt="A large abstract painting in pink, green and ochre hanging on a shop wall, next to a shelf of snacks" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-09.jpg" alt="A painted canvas hung among cereal boxes on a bodega shelf" /%}
