Montez Press Radio introduces another spatial condition by temporarily transforming a moving environment into a radio broadcast station. During the February 2025 Tắm Đêm event, the radio station moved from its usual studio onto a boat traveling along the Saigon River. The boat became a studio, performance space, and gathering place at the same time. By moving outside the traditional studio, Montez Press Radio created a new atmosphere and energy for the broadcast, allowing the boat's movement, the sound of the water and engines, the voices of participants, and the changing landscape to become part of the broadcast experience.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/kaela-12.jpg" alt="People seated on red stools under the canopy of a boat on the Saigon River at dusk, during the Montez Press Radio broadcast" /%}

These case studies suggest a model in which removing the typical gallery can create new relationships between artists, audiences, and communities. By placing art in existing spaces and infrastructure,  the space can respond to its surroundings, engage with existing communities, and change from one context to another. Social media and digital platforms extend these encounters beyond the physical space, building digital followings that allow people to view the exhibition long after they have ended and follow upcoming ones. This creates an interesting relationship between art that is accidentally encountered vs sought out by an already established following. By making art less dependent on fixed cultural institutions, these projects can expand access, give artists more freedom to experiment, and create opportunities for art and artists to engage directly with the communities they occupy.

